  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Night-Shift Readout",
    "overview": "A monitor glowing at three in the morning. The ground is Midnight Underpass;\npanels sit on it one shade lighter, framed by a single Deep Circuit line. The\nnumbers are what you came for, and they read cold and exact — Static Cyan for a\nvalue, Ghostly Green for health, Signal Gold for what you can spend. When\nsomething goes wrong, Neon Veil magenta says so, and when a hit lands it flashes\nonce. The rest of the time the screen is quiet.\n\nIt is an instrument, not a poster. Density and legibility win over atmosphere,\nbecause the sheet is read mid-scene on a phone between turns. Atmosphere lives in\nprecise details: letter-spaced mono labels, a violet haze behind the header, the\ndisplay face on a character's name, the magenta hatching on a Health Level that\nMassive damage took away. Neon is signal — state, damage, focus, selection —\nnever decoration.\n\nIt is **not** generic cyberpunk (no glitch, scanlines, chromatic aberration or\nterminal green), **not** a fantasy parchment sheet (no scrolls, textures or\nornament, magic or not), and **not** a SaaS dashboard (no big soft cards,\npastel gradients or friendly illustration). Shadows is noir and urban fantasy.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Dark midnight-navy ground; depth by tone, one 1px frame line per surface.",
      "Engineered at rest, colour only in response: hover, selection, state, damage.",
      "Four type voices, each with one job: display for names, Inter for prose, mono for labels and ids, Oxanium for big numbers.",
      "Small, consistent radii (3–8px); nothing pill-soft except the jump chips.",
      "Colour carries fixed meaning: cyan value/focus, green health/done, gold spend/caution, magenta harm/error, violet the brand's hand.",
      "A light theme exists and folds the neons onto the two brand constants rather than inventing new colours."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Signal-Not-Decor Rule",
        "body": "A neon appears only when it means something: a value, a state, a warning, harm, focus. If you can't name what the colour is telling the player, it's Overcast Steel.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Meaning Rule",
        "body": "Each neon has one job and keeps it on every screen. Magenta never marks something good; green never marks something spent. A new readout takes the colour of what it *is* (a pool of Luck is gold wherever it appears).",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Fold Rule",
        "body": "In the light theme, cyan and green fold onto Deep Circuit and gold and magenta fold onto Aether Pulse, because the neons fail contrast on a pale ground. Never hard-code a neon hex; use the token so the fold happens.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Four Voices Rule",
        "body": "Display names things, Inter explains them, mono labels and counts them, Oxanium shows the number you came for. Don't cross them: no Inter labels in capitals, no display face in a paragraph, no big numbers in Inter.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Mono Caps Rule",
        "body": "Every label that names a field or a section is Roboto Mono, uppercase, letter-spaced at least .12em, in Overcast Steel (Aether Pulse, lit for section rules). It's the system's handwriting.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tonal Floor Rule",
        "body": "Surfaces at rest are flat. A shadow means the thing floats (tip, toast, modal, drawer); a glow means the thing just changed state (selected, hit, pulse). Nothing gets a shadow for looking important.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One-Line Frame Rule",
        "body": "A surface is a tonal step plus one 1px Deep Circuit line. State recolours the line (cyan selected or worn, green picked, magenta danger, violet on hover); it doesn't thicken it, except on the Pain bands, where weight *is* the meaning.",
        "section": "shapes"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** use the tokens (`--cyan`, `--magenta`, …), never the hex, so the light theme's fold happens.",
      "**Do** give every new number the colour of what it is — gold for anything spent, green for health, cyan for a plain value, magenta only for harm.",
      "**Do** label fields and sections in Mono Caps: Roboto Mono, uppercase, .12em or more, Overcast Steel.",
      "**Do** frame every surface with one 1px Deep Circuit line and change the line's colour for state.",
      "**Do** keep motion short (.15–.35s, ease-out) and state-driven, and turn every animation off under `prefers-reduced-motion`.",
      "**Do** focus every control with the 2px Static Cyan outline.",
      "**Do** keep text on a violet or Deep Circuit fill `--on-accent` (white) in both themes."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** use neon as decoration: no glowing headers, gradient text, neon borders on resting surfaces, or colour that means nothing.",
      "**Don't** reach for generic cyberpunk: glitch, scanlines, chromatic aberration, terminal green, Matrix rain.",
      "**Don't** reach for fantasy parchment: scrolls, paper texture, ornament, blackletter — not even on the Magic tab.",
      "**Don't** drift toward a SaaS dashboard: big rounded cards, soft pastel gradients, friendly illustration, radii past 8px.",
      "**Don't** put a shadow on a surface at rest; shadows are for things that float.",
      "**Don't** use Neon Veil for anything good, or Ghostly Green for anything spent.",
      "**Don't** set big numbers in Inter or prose in the display face.",
      "**Don't** use a raw neon as text in the light theme; it fails contrast. The fold exists for this."
    ]
  }
}
